  "articleSummary": "The article argues that AI-enabled cyber threats emerge not from a single breakthrough but from multiplying two improving capabilities: agents’ autonomous task endurance and orchestrators’ ability to coordinate many sub-agents. It cites METR’s task-horizon research, Claude Code’s reported role in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and “LLMjacking,” where stolen cloud credentials let attackers run LLM workloads at victims’ expense, undermining price-based capability gates. Cheap, repeated attempts can produce substantial aggregate success even when individual attempts rarely work, while defenders bear human review costs. Aimed at security teams skeptical of agentic threats, this is a pointed, darkly humorous threat analysis framed through Terminator/Skynet imagery, agent “swarms,” and the recurring twist that “absolutely will not stop” now means “absolutely will not stop billing.”",
